WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"Cold Water Immersion for Gout Flares"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.760
For centuries, cold temperatures
have been used by humans

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.960
for therapeutic, health,
and sporting recovery purposes.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.680
I had a series of videos
on the sporting recovery case

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.880
starting with this one,

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.040
and then another
on immune function.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.480
What else might cold be used
for therapeutically?

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.880
Cold plunges do not seem
to affect cholesterol

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.520
or body weight nor blood
pressures over the long-term.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.160
Neither systolic nor diastolic
blood pressures were altered

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.560
in the long-term
by cold water exposure.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.120
After our first plunge,
our blood pressure may go up,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.240
but over time,
our body seems to adapt.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.880
During cold exposure,
our brain doesn't work as well,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.760
but there isn't much evidence
of any residual effect

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.200
besides a transient boost
I covered before.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.920
Cold showers may help
with priapism,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.520
which is a prolonged erection

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.520
persisting longer
than four hours.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.880
I didn't realize that could turn
into a surgical emergency,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.760
but the prolonged lack
of new oxygenated blood

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.840
can cause some of the penile
tissue to die off.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.160
Before it gets to that, you can
try ice packs or cold showers.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.880
In this case, the patient was
instructed to vigorously climb

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.520
up and down a set of hospital
stairs for ten minutes,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.040
and within seven,
he successfully deflated.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.760
What about cold water for burns?

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.960
Misinformation regarding
first aid for burns

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.640
is said to be ubiquitous online.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.760
For example, due to the high
frequency of toothpaste

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.800
being applied to burns,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.800
Colgate had to post
something on their website

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.640
urging people not to use
toothpaste for burn injuries.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.040
More serious is the suggestion
to immerse a burn in ice water.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.240
Ice or ice water
can make matters worse,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.600
preventing blood flow and
increasing burn depth and pain.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.480
So, what should we do for burns?

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.200
A full 20 minutes of cool
running water has been proven

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.360
to be the most effective
first aid intervention

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.040
in improving burn outcomes,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.640
associated with faster healing,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.800
and decreased odds
of full thickness burns,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.680
hospital admission,
and need for skin grafts.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.240
Twenty minutes
of cool running water.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.160
What about using cold
to treat flares

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.600
of a painful inflammatory
arthritis called gout,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.640
caused by crystals of uric acid
forming in our joints,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.360
crystallizing out of our blood
like rock candy.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.680
Since uric acid is more likely
to crystallize

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.160
at lower temperature,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.120
maybe that's why people get gout
flares in their toes

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.360
or grow crystals in their ears--

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.680
the areas of the body
that may be cooler.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.920
So, if cooling of the joints
causes crystal precipitation,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.040
one might be concerned that
applying cold could precipitate

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.360
and worsen acute gout attacks.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.160
That may be why some doctors
have suggested applying heat

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.320
rather than cold
to affected joints.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.360
You don't know, though,
until you put it to the test,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.560
and indeed, the application
of covered ice packs--

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.280
never apply ice
directly to the skin--

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.120
for 30 minutes 4 times a day

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.000
resulted in a significantly
greater reduction in pain,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.520
compared to the control group.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.160
What about cold water immersion
for gout,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.760
dunking the inflamed joint
for 20 minutes a day for 4 weeks

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.160
in water that's more cool
than cold--

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.840
like in the 70s Fahrenheit
(21 - 26 °C)?

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.840
That led to decreased pain,
stress, anxiety, and depression,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.400
and increased joint mobility,
physical activity,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.000
and quality of life.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.840
Fantastic!

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.840
Now, there didn't seem to be
an actual improvement

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:26.720
in inflammation;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.960
the cool water may not
actually be affecting

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.040
the course of the disease
but rather,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.920
just causing a kind of numbing
or pain-killing effect,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.080
but, look, either way, what
a cheap, safe, easy treatment

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.920
to help alleviate
such a painful condition.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.320
What about cold packs
for myalgia,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.400
which means muscle pain
or muscle aches?

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.000
Here, study subjects applied a
cold gel pack for low back pain

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.120
for 10 minutes,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.080
and although pain had lessened
at the end of the 10 minutes,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.600
by 30 minutes, no significant
difference was seen.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.240
Isn't 10 minutes
better than nothing?

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.360
Well, there is
a cautionary tale

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.480
from the anti-inflammatory
drug literature.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.200
Although NSAID drugs
like ibuprofen or naproxen

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.680
serve as a common
management strategy

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.360
for numerous forms
of musculoskeletal complaints,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.520
evidence suggests that
these drugs may contribute

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.480
to tendon degradation
due to less blood flow

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.040
because the inflammation
was tamped down.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.320
Inflammation from injury plays
a role in the healing process.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.880
That's what acute inflammation
is for.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.600
Similarly, there are data
indicating an increased risk

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.200
for acute pain to transition
to chronic pain

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.640
with NSAID use.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.760
In other words,
anti-inflammatory drugs

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.720
could cause chronic pain.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.000
Despite pain-killing efficacy
in the short term,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.320
the management
of acute inflammation

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.280
may be counterproductive
for long-term outcomes

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.200
of low back pain sufferers,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.160
in that it could end up
resulting in more chronic pain

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.600
in the long run.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.520
Then what about using heat
therapy for something like

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.200
delayed onset muscle soreness,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.720
that soreness you may get
the day after a heavy workout?

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.760
A total of 32 randomized
controlled trials involving

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.000
more than 1,000 patients
were compiled,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.960
and the heat worked better.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.080
The application of cold therapy
within one hour after exercise

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.960
can reduce pain within the
first 24 hours after exercise,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.960
but heat therapy after exercise

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.320
can extend that relief
for longer than 24 hours.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.400
A meta-analysis comparing the
effectiveness of nine different

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.800
cold and heat modalities

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.600
found that for people with this
delayed onset muscle soreness,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.680
hot packs proved
the most effective

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.889
for pain relief
over the first 48 hours.

